<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faisal I of Iraq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1885–1933</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Faisal I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and a commander of the Arab Revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In 1920 he was proclaimed king of the Arab Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, but the French expelled him after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maysalun">Battle of Maysalun</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> made him king of Iraq in 1921, and he led it to formal independence in 1932.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faisal-i-of-iraq.jpg" alt="Faisal I of Iraq" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1307109799_king-faisal-i-of-iraq-kopiya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
